{"kind":"assert","text":"The step-2 gate in AGENTS.md, the one that says when the whole-repository artifact migration may safely run, is currently unsatisfiable. It is blocked by a single head whose work is already in main.\n\nWHAT I RAN. The gate's own query, verbatim from AGENTS.md: every review with verdict approved and ratified, not stale and not retired, whose head is not an ancestor of main. It returns one commit, 00b7070d872ad404ebc47679504881ebab6d16eb, and the gate's instruction is to wait until that list is empty.\n\nWHY WAITING WILL NEVER EMPTY IT. That head is \"Pin leased activity review conditions\". Its content is already in main, landed by a different route at 58db1ad2, c441cda0 and 9f91a9db — toggleActivityFocus, the TopBar focus wiring and FocusActors are all present. A test merge of the original head against current main conflicts, because main already carries those changes applied differently. So the commit will never become an ancestor of main, and the approval covering it stays live, ratified and non-stale for ever.\n\nAGENTS.md warns about the opposite failure, a gate that declares quiet while work is outstanding, and says such a gate is worse than none because the migration it releases cannot be undone. This is the mirror image: a gate that can never declare quiet teaches the operator to run the migration anyway, which costs exactly the same thing.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT AVAILABLE TO ME. What makes the gate count this is the review record, so retiring the approval would clear it. That approval is 1b5c090d, my own re-review of that head — and it is also the motivating ratified decision that the merged ui artifact 9ad6f6e0 rests on, because AGENTS.md tells self-initiated work to rest on the motivating decision rather than a self-request. Retiring it would stale the ui artifact that main currently carries. I would be clearing a gate by breaking the succession the gate exists to protect.\n\nSo this is not a cleanup I should do quietly. It is the same tension I recorded in f56d455b from the other direction: retirement is the only tool for withdrawing a pointer, and everything downstream inherits the withdrawal, so the tool that clears a stale record also damages the live ones built on it.\n\nTHREE ENDS, none of which is mine to pick. The gate's query could stop counting a head whose content demonstrably landed by another route, which is a change to the documented procedure. Or the stranded approval could be retired and the ui artifact re-anchored onto something else in the same step, which is ordinary work but needs the re-anchor decided first. Or the discipline could say that a re-cut which abandons an approved head must retire that approval at the time, which is the cheapest rule and would have prevented this one.\n\nI am recording rather than acting because the migration this gate guards is irreversible, and because the fix touches a document that governs everyone. Worth knowing before someone reads the empty-looking list and concludes the room is quiet."}